"\n## Linux DRM - Code Flow of open() and ioctl()\n\n### Introduction\n| Part | Topic |\n|---|---|\n| Part 1 | Architecture and the Big Picture |\n| Part 2 | Core Initialization and Driver Registration |\n\nIn Part 2, we looked at the process of creating nodes such as /dev/dri/card0. This post traces the kernel functions executed the moment a user uses that node—specifically through open() and ioctl(). Finally, we will look at an example performing the same tasks in user space using libdrm.  \n\n\n\n\n### Code Flow 3: open()\nWhen a user calls open(\"/dev/dri/card0\"), the kernel looks up the stub fops previously registered with major number 226. The stub does not perform real work itself; instead, it swaps out the file operations table with the driver's own.  \n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_drv.c L1188-L1216 (Linux v6.19)\n\n\nThe .open hook held in the driver's fops is drm_open(). This comes from a single macro line.  \n\n\n\n> **Source** include/drm/drm_gem.h L464-L473 (Linux v6.19)\n\nInside this macro lie all the entry points we will see throughout this series: .unlocked_ioctl = drm_ioctl (ioctl), .read = drm_read (reading events), .mmap = drm_gem_mmap (buffer mapping), and .poll = drm_poll. Here are drm_open() and drm_open_helper():  \n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_file.c L369-L403 (Linux v6.19)\n\ndrm_open() resolves the minor and forwards it to drm_open_helper().  \n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_file.c L316-L355 (Linux v6.19)\n\ndrm_file_alloc() allocates the state corresponding to a single open(). The object_idr mentioned earlier, the event queue, and other fields are initialized here.  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_file.c L132-L197 (Linux v6.19)\n\ndrm_gem_open() initializes the handle table, which internally calls idr_init_base(&file_private->object_idr, 1). This is the reason why handle numbers start from 1 (leaving 0 to represent \"none\"). Thanks to filp->private_data = priv, subsequent ioctl, read, and mmap calls can directly obtain drm_file from file->private_data.  \n\n### Code Flow 4: ioctl()\nioctl(fd, DRM_IOCTL_xxx, arg) enters into drm_ioctl(), which serves as fops->unlocked_ioctl.  \n\n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_ioctl.c L821-L919 (Linux v6.19)\n\nSummarizing the core steps:  \n\n1. Checks whether the type of the ioctl number is 'd' (DRM_IOCTL_BASE).\n2. If the number is 0x40 or greater, it looks up the entry in the driver table (dev->driver->ioctls); otherwise, it looks in the core table (drm_ioctls[]).\n3. Rather than trusting the size provided by the user, it calculates the buffer size using the kernel definition (ioctl->cmd) (Do not trust userspace).\n4. After copy_from_user(), drm_ioctl_kernel() checks permissions and invokes the actual function.\n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_ioctl.c L787-L805 (Linux v6.19)\n\nThe core ioctl table is an array constructed using macros. Selecting only the entries we will encounter in this series yields the following list. The flags on the far right correspond to the permission rules mentioned earlier.  \n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_ioctl.c L632-L735 (Linux v6.19)\n\n\n\n\n### libdrm: The View from User Space\nNow let's trace the flow above directly from user space. The following program opens a DRM device, prints driver details, and lists KMS objects.  \n\n\nEach libdrm function maps directly to a single kernel ioctl. You can match the exact names from the drm_ioctls[] table in the code above:  \n\n| libdrm Function | ioctl | Kernel Handler | Flags |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| drmGetVersion() | DRM_IOCTL_VERSION | drm_version() | RENDER_ALLOW |\n| drmGetCap() | DRM_IOCTL_GET_CAP | drm_getcap() | RENDER_ALLOW |\n| drmSetClientCap() | DRM_IOCTL_SET_CLIENT_CAP | drm_setclientcap() | None |\n| drmModeGetResources() | DRM_IOCTL_MODE_GETRESOURCES | drm_mode_getresources() | None |\n| drmModeGetConnector() | DRM_IOCTL_MODE_GETCONNECTOR | drm_mode_getconnector() | None |\n| drmModeGetPlaneResources() | DRM_IOCTL_MODE_GETPLANERESOURCES | drm_mode_getplane_res() | None |\n| drmModeObjectGetProperties() | DRM_IOCTL_MODE_OBJ_GETPROPERTIES | drm_mode_obj_get_properties_ioctl() | None |\n\nThis program has been verified to compile with libdrm 2.4.129. Running it requires access permissions to /dev/dri/card0 (usually available in a desktop login session, or requires membership in the video/render group). Running strace -e trace=openat,ioctl ./drm_probe shows the sequence of ioctls invoked by the user (depending on the strace version, it will also display decoded names such as DRM_IOCTL_VERSION).  \n\n### Code Locations\n| Step | Function | Location (Linux v6.19) |\n|---|---|---|\n| open: Create file state | drm_open_helper() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_file.c:316 |\n| open: Allocate drm_file | drm_file_alloc() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_file.c:132 |\n| open: Set master | drm_master_open() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_auth.c:317 |\n| ioctl: Entry and dispatch | drm_ioctl() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_ioctl.c:821 |\n| ioctl: Check permissions | drm_ioctl_permit() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_ioctl.c:599 |\n| ioctl: Core ioctl table | drm_ioctls | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_ioctl.c:632 |\n\n\n\n\n### Summary\nHere is what we covered in this post:  \n\n- open: The stub replaces the fops with the driver's own, and drm_file_alloc() creates the state (handle table, event queue) for a single open().\n- ioctl: drm_ioctl() locates the entry in the core or driver table, and drm_ioctl_permit() validates permissions before calling the handler.\n- Most libdrm functions are thin wrappers around these ioctl calls.\n\nNext up is rendering. First, we will examine how GEM handles stored in drm_file's object_idr are created, and how their buffers are read and written by the CPU."